{
  "_comment": "Antigravity (agy) permission baseline. Merged by setup.sh into ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json via claude/scripts/agy-apply-permissions.py (that file also holds trustedWorkspaces and per-machine grants, so it is never symlinked). Rules are word-by-word prefixes using unified command(...) syntax (agy retired unsandboxed(...) rules). Precedence: deny > ask > allow. Security model: the terminal sandbox is the boundary (toolPermission=proceed-in-sandbox, enableTerminalSandbox=true); it blocks ~/.ssh and .env files by default and isolates the filesystem and network. Only read-only gh subcommands and local dev/test commands are in baseline allow; git push and gh write commands prompt outside the sandbox unless granted per machine. Reads outside the workspace are served inside the sandbox through allowNonWorkspaceAccess and read_file(~/...) rules, which the merge script expands to the machine's home; the baseline holds no read_file(~/...) rules for $HOME symlinks that resolve into ~/dev/ (such as ~/.bash_aliases or ~/.gitconfig), because bwrap resolves realpath on read_file rules, so such a rule would mount its target read-only on top of the read-write ~/dev/ mount. read_file denies cover credential files exactly, because the file tool matches paths, not shell text. Withdrawing a rule from the permissions object does not withdraw it from a machine that already installed it, because apply is additive; the retired array lists grants the baseline has revoked. apply deletes any exact match in retired from allow and migrates/strips legacy unsandboxed(...) rules while preserving operator ask and deny restrictions; check fails until retired and legacy unsandboxed rules are gone. There are deliberately no command regexes: shell syntax cannot be classified airtight (see the reverted #411), so command denies are plain prefixes that act as speed bumps inside the sandbox, not as a boundary.",
